    ":question/math-academy-id": "e-5926",
    ":question/problem": "![](images/fb/fb46d056a8d8010eca509543c231c875f4980a21f1af17505fa98c8c64106207.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the graph of $y = \\dfrac{a}{x-b}+k.$ What is the value of $a+b+k?$",
    ":question/worked-solution": "First, notice that the horizontal asymptote of the graph is $y=-1,$ while the vertical asymptote is $x=2.$ This means that $k=-1$ and $b=2,$ and we can re-write our function as\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{a}{x-2}+(-1) = \\dfrac{a}{x-2}-1.\n$$\n\nAlso, we are given that the point $(3,2)$ lies on the graph. Substituting its coordinates into the equation above, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{a}{x-2}-1 \\\\ 2 & =\\frac{a}{3-2}-1 \\\\ 3 & =\\frac{a}{1} \\\\ a & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a+b+k = 3+2+(-1) = 4.$"
  },
