      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-113479",
      ":question/problem": "![](images/65/65348161e386fac14a98e8440d32d3883b5865a6d1eecfcd58e12931730ad0b8.png)\n\nThe graph represents the function $y=\\frac{a}{x-b}+k.$ What is the value of $a+b+k?$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "First, notice that the horizontal asymptote of the graph is $y=2,$ while the vertical asymptote is $x=1.$ This means that $k=2$ and $b=1,$ and we can re-write our function as\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{a}{x-1}+2.\n$$\n\nAlso, we are given that the point $(2,5)$ lies on the graph. Substituting its coordinates into the equation above, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{a}{x-1}+2 \\\\ 5 & =\\frac{a}{2-1}+2 \\\\ 3 & =\\frac{a}{1} \\\\ a & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a+b+k=3+1+2=6.$"
    },
