      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-113482",
      ":question/problem": "![](images/4d/4d2dec8a05bc9835693c1df9dbc54618fd39d77667cbbdcd5bb6d92bf25c0d97.png)\n\nThe graph represents the function $y=\\frac{a}{x-b}+k.$ What is the value of $a+b+k?$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "First, notice that the horizontal asymptote of the graph is $y=0.5,$ while the vertical asymptote is $x=3.$ This means that $k=0.5$ and $b=3,$ and we can re-write our function as\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{a}{x-3}+0.5.\n$$\n\nAlso, we are given that the point $(1,1.5)$ lies on the graph. Substituting its coordinates into the equation above, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{a}{x-3}+0.5 \\\\ 1.5 & =\\frac{a}{1-3}+0.5 \\\\ 1 & =\\frac{a}{-2} \\\\ a & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a+b+k=-2+3+0.5=1.5.$"
    },
