      ":question/problem": "![](images/c9/c90af67d867e33c847f66721c1abbc6768fbc34e7f39648fbc2e4f486a87ca3f.png)\n\nThe graph represents the function $y=\\frac{a}{x-b}+k.$ What are the values of $a,$ $b,$ and $k?$\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}},$\\,b=$\n{{field-2}},$\\,k=$\n{{field-3}}",
      ":question/worked-solution": "First, notice that the horizontal asymptote of the graph is $y=2.5,$ while the vertical asymptote is $x=-2.$ This means that $k=2.5$ and $b=-2,$ and we can re-write our function as\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{a}{x-(-2)}+2.5=\\frac{a}{x+2}+2.5.\n$$\n\nAlso, we are given that the point $(-5,3.5)$ lies on the graph. Substituting its coordinates into the equation above, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{a}{x+2}+2.5 \\\\ 3.5 & =\\frac{a}{-5+2}+2.5 \\\\ 1 & =\\frac{a}{(-3)} \\\\ a & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"
    }
  ],
  ":knowledge-point/title": "Solving for Some Unknowns Given the Graph of a Transformed Reciprocal Function"
}
